{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011FYg2Np12z7nLaw5qRydvD","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment 1 2 3 4 5 scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Dashboard\",\"url\":\"https://stats.aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Action Editor Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"}]\n\nBased on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, I found the review form with the 1-5 scale assessment information. Here's what the overall assessment scale looks like:\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale (1-5)\n\nThe scale includes:\n- **5 = Excellent**: This study is one of the most thorough seen, given its type\n- **4 = Strong**: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims\n- **3 = Acceptable**: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\n- **2 = Poor**: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported, with major technical/methodological problems\n- **1 = Major Issues**: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication or is not relevant to ACL\n\nAdditionally, the overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, and is a composite score reflecting assessment of soundness, excitement, and other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72bdb13e-4703-43e0-b0b0-f41e588f3f9b","uuid":"14d13569-5d1d-43ab-bf4a-8591e08d0b57","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:29:56.205Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment 1 2 3 4 5 scale","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GzaFWeAU3RpGD5yfdqDk2t","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Dashboard","url":"https://stats.aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Action Editor Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"}]},"Based on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, I found the review form with the 1-5 scale assessment information. Here's what the overall assessment scale looks like:\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale (1-5)\n\nThe scale includes:\n- **5 = Excellent**: This study is one of the most thorough seen, given its type\n- **4 = Strong**: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims\n- **3 = Acceptable**: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\n- **2 = Poor**: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported, with major technical/methodological problems\n- **1 = Major Issues**: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication or is not relevant to ACL\n\nAdditionally, the overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper if it were committed to an *ACL venue, and is a composite score reflecting assessment of soundness, excitement, and other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":5.291463067000004}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SL5Ytgb9jARLqJa7gMXZMa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form information. Let me also fetch the WebFetch tool to get the exact review form details from aclrollingreview.org/reviewform.\n\nActually, I have enough information. The ARR review form (used by EMNLP 2026) uses:\n\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 scale\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 scale  \n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 scale (composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact)\n\nWhere:\n- 5 = Top (excellent/highly exciting)\n\n\n- 4 = Strong/Exciting\n- 3 = Acceptable/Borderline\n- 2 = Poor/Below threshold\n- 1 = Major issues\n\nEMNLP acceptance rate is typically ~22-25%.\n\nNow I'm ready to start the review process. I'll dispatch both reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then bring them together for the roundtable discussion where they'll share findings, identify agreements and disagreements, and converge on a final assessment before I synthesize everything into the review report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":776,"cache_read_input_tokens":62094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":776},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72bdb13e-4703-43e0-b0b0-f41e588f3f9b","uuid":"e57674de-91e4-4058-b7c1-f1bb90bb80ac"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SL5Ytgb9jARLqJa7gMXZMa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have the EMNLP 2026 / ARR review criteria. EMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review with three scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), and Overall Assessment (1-5). Dispatching two reviewers in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":776,"cache_read_input_tokens":62094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":776},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72bdb13e-4703-43e0-b0b0-f41e588f3f9b","uuid":"e9a2ee8a-c44b-40a5-aa99-d5bf4122decc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SL5Ytgb9jARLqJa7gMXZMa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U6ErCXqTustc7s6d8dbtet","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**FREIGE: Faithful Relation Extraction with Evidence via Contrastive Evidence Discrimination**\n\nThe paper addresses a gap in LLM-based document-level relation extraction (DocRE): existing LLM approaches produce no verifiable evidence for predicted relations. FREIGE is the first LLM-based DocRE system that jointly generates relation triples and supporting evidence sentences.\n\nKey contributions:\n1. **CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination)**: A scoring function computing m = p_pos - p_neg, contrasting NLI entailment of gold evidence vs best entity-sharing distractor. Uses DeBERTa-v3-base on MNLI. Discrimination accuracy 81.5%, mean margin 0.48.\n2. **RSFT pipeline**: SFT model generates N=8 candidates per doc → CED scores → top-k=1 retained (~2700 from ~21000, 12.8% selection rate) → second LoRA fine-tuning (1 epoch, α=128, lr=2e-5).\n3. **GRPO catastrophic failure**: Online RL with CED/flat-NLI/extraction-only rewards all fail — reward rises monotonically while rel-F1 collapses to <2 within 10 steps. Claimed as first empirical demonstration of reward overoptimization in structured IE.\n4. **EDCR metric**: Entity-sharing Distractor Citation Rate = fraction of cited sentences that share entities but aren't gold evidence. Pearson r = -0.42 with Evi-F1.\n\n## Main Results (from paper)\n- **Test set (Table 1)**: RSFT+CED: rel-F1=45.4, ign-F1=45.2, Evi-F1=80.2, EDCR=54.8\n- Encoder SOTA DREEAM: rel-F1=63.3, Evi-F1=57.3 (attention-based evidence, different protocol)\n- SFT baseline: rel-F1=39.7, Evi-F1=78.9, EDCR=70.5\n- RSFT flat-NLI: rel-F1=42.9, Evi-F1=78.5, EDCR=68.6\n\n**Dev set ablations (Table 2)**: CED RSFT outperforms flat-NLI RSFT: +2.8pp rel-F1, +2.6pp Evi-F1, -5.8pp EDCR\n\n**EDCR diagnostic (Table 3)**: GRPO ckpt-90 has Evi-F1=56.7 but EDCR=85.8 (catastrophic distractor). CED reranker: EDCR=49.8 (best). Pearson r=-0.42.\n\n**DREEAM reproduction (Table 4)**: Gap <0.3pp for rel-F1 and Evi-F1 on dev.\n\n**CED reranker (Table 5)**: rel-F1=39.8, Evi-F1=80.5, EDCR=49.8, no extra training, 8x inference.\n\n**GRPO trajectory (Table 6)**: SFT init rel-F1=40.6 → step-10: 0.3, step-100: 1.4. Reward: 0.45→1.20. With KL=0.01: rel-F1=21.1, EDCR=85.8.\n\n**Model size ablation (Table 7)**: Qwen3-1.7B/4B/8B all benefit from RSFT+CED. Δrel-F1 = +3.6/+6.0/+4.4pp.\n\n**Re-DocRED (Table 8)**: RSFT+CED advantage maintained (+4.6pp rel-F1, +1.9pp Evi-F1).\n\n**Top-k ablation (Table 9)**: k=1 slightly better than k=3 (+0.8pp rel-F1, +0.5pp Evi-F1, -1.7pp EDCR).\n\n**NLI model ablation (Table 10)**: DeBERTa-base vs large: marginal improvement (+0.6pp Evi-F1, -1.7pp EDCR). Base sufficient.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review context)\nThe review context lists 10 imagined experiments. The paper covers:\n- ✅ CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation (plan_001)\n- ✅ Test set evaluation (plan_002)\n- ✅ EDCR cross-method diagnostic (plan_003)\n- ✅ DREEAM baseline reproduction (plan_004)\n- ✅ CED-as-reranker (plan_005)\n- ✅ Model size ablation (plan_006)\n- ✅ Re-DocRED extension (plan_007)\n- ✅ top_k ablation (plan_008)\n- ✅ GRPO forgetting dynamics (plan_009)\n- ✅ NLI model ablation (plan_010)\n\nAll 10 experiments appear covered.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ACL Rolling Review)\nScoring: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5)\n- 5=Excellent/Top, 4=Strong/Exciting, 3=Acceptable/Borderline, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues\n- Acceptance rate ~22-25%\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is CED well-motivated? Is the RSFT pipeline rigorous? Is the GRPO analysis thorough? Is EDCR well-defined?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are baselines fair? Are ablations sufficient? Are statistical claims supported? Any missing experiments?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided?\n4. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**: Be specific with line/table references.\n\nStructure your output as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: Major/Minor)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":776,"cache_read_input_tokens":62094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":776},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72bdb13e-4703-43e0-b0b0-f41e588f3f9b","uuid":"81135d45-9d6a-4b40-89cc-a5f8a7764daf"}
